<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 2.6 - The geometry of matrix transformations</title>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Matrix transformation perform geometric operations.
                Go to
                <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0Jf" visual="gvsu.edu/s/0Jf">this website</url>
                to study the effect that various matrix transformations have on
                the plane.
                On the left is the plane before the transformation; on the right
                is the plane after the transformation.
            </p>

            <p>
                Describe the geometric effect of the matrix transformations
                defined by
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Scales by 2 in the <m>x</m>-direction.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(a).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that scales by 2 in the
                        x-direction.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Scales by 2 in both the <m>x</m>-direction and
                    <m>y</m>-direction.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(b).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that scales by 2 in both the
                        x-direction and y-direction.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Rotates by <m>90^o</m> counterclockwise.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(c).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that rotates by 90 degrees
                        counterclockwise.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Skews to the right.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(d).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that skews to the right.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}-1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Flips over the <m>y</m>-axis.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(e).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that flips over the y--axis.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Projects (condenses) on to the <m>x</m>-axis.
                    Note: columns are not linearly independent.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(f).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that projects (condenses) on to
                        the x-axis.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Nothing!
                    This is the identity matrix!
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(g).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that does nothing!
                        This is the identity matrix!
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{cc}1 \amp -1 \\ -2 \amp 2\end{array}\right]</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Projects onto a line and stretches.
                    Note: columns are not linearly independent.
                </p>

                <image source="images/14-Sec2_6-1(h).png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Linear transformation that projects onto a line and
                        stretches.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Transformations that scale.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix <m>A</m> that defines the transformation
                    <m>T</m> that scales all vectors horizontally by <m>3</m>
                    and vertically by <m>5</m>.
                    You can go to
                    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0Jf" visual="gvsu.edu/s/0Jf">this website</url>
                    to check your work (with smaller numbers)!
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let's think about the action we want with respect to our
                    vectors <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                    First, we want to take <m>\evec_1 = \twovec{1}{0}</m> and
                    scale this horizontally by 3 (note: the vertical component
                    is zero here).
                    This means if we input <m>\twovec{1}{0}</m>, the output
                    should be <m>\twovec{3}{0}</m> to scale by 3 horizontally
                    (1st coordinate).
                </p>

                <p>
                    Then, for <m>\evec_2 = \twovec{0}{1}</m> and scale this
                    verically by 5 (note: the horizontal component is zero
                    here).
                    This means if we input <m>\twovec{0}{1}</m>, the output
                    should be <m>\twovec{0}{5}</m> to scale by 5 vertically (2nd
                    coordinate).
                </p>

                <p>
                    Therefore, we have
                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix} </m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In general, what is the matrix that that will define the
                    transformation that scales all vectors horizontally by
                    <m>a</m> and vertically by <m>b</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \begin{bmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp b \end{bmatrix}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What matrix produces a <m>180^o</m> rotation?
                (Find <m>T(\evec_1)</m> and <m>T(\evec_2)</m> to find the matrix
                - you can check it using the applet.)
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Again, we look at the action of the transformation on our
                vectors <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                So,
                <md>
                    T(\evec_1) = T\left(\twovec{1}{0}\right) = \twovec{-1}{0} \\
            T(\evec_2) = T\left(\twovec{0}{1}\right) = \twovec{0}{-1} \\
                </md>
                Therefore, the matrix that produces this transformation is
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What matrix produces a reflection over the line <m>y = -x</m>?
                (Find <m>T(\evec_1)</m> and <m>T(\evec_2)</m> to find the matrix
                - you can check it using the applet.)
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Again, we look at the action of the transformation on our
                vectors <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                So,
                <md>
                    T(\evec_1) = T\left(\twovec{1}{0}\right) = \twovec{0}{-1} \\
            T(\evec_2) = T\left(\twovec{0}{1}\right) = \twovec{-1}{0} \\
                </md>
                Therefore, the matrix that produces this transformation is
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                All of the transformations in this question have the form
                <m>T: \real^2 \to \real^2</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix of the transformation that has no effect on
                    vectors; that is, <m>T(\xvec) = \xvec</m>.
                    We call this matrix the identity and denote it by <m>I</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We saw in Problem 1(g) that the identity matrix has no
                    transformational effect on vectors.
                    Additionally, note that <m>I\xvec = \xvec</m>.
                    Therefore, <m>{T(\xvec) = \xvec = I\xvec}</m>, such that the
                    corresponding matrix for this transformation is
                    <m>{I = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.}</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix <m>B</m> of the transformation that reflects
                    vectors in <m>\real^2</m> over the line <m>y=x</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Again, we look at the action of the transformation on our
                    vectors <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                    So,
                    <md>
                        T(\evec_1) = T\left(\twovec{1}{0}\right) = \twovec{0}{1} \\
            T(\evec_2) = T\left(\twovec{0}{1}\right) = \twovec{1}{0} \\
                    </md>
                    Therefore, the matrix that produces this transformation is
                    <md>
                        B = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the result of composing the reflection you found in
                    the previous part with itself; that is, what is the effect
                    of reflecting in the line <m>y=x</m> and then reflecting in
                    this line again.
                    Provide a geometric explanation for your result as well as
                    an algebraic one obtained by multiplying matrices.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    This corresponds to
                    <md>
                        B^2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} = \left[\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}\twovec{0}{1} \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,  \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}\twovec{1}{0}\right] \\
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} = I.
                    </md>
                    Thus, reflecting twice returns to the original vector.
                    This makes geometric sense!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                More composing transformations.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix <m>C</m> that rotates vectors
                    counterclockwise in the plane by <m>90^\circ</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We look at the action of the transformation on our vectors
                    <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                    So,
                    <md>
                        T(\evec_1) = T\left(\twovec{1}{0}\right) = \twovec{0}{1} \\
            T(\evec_2) = T\left(\twovec{0}{1}\right) = \twovec{-1}{0} \\
                    </md>
                    Therefore, the matrix that produces this transformation is
                    <md>
                        C = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix that results from composing a
                    <m>90^\circ</m> rotation with itself.
                    Explain the geometric meaning of this operation.
                    Find the matrix that results from composing a
                    <m>90^\circ</m> rotation with itself four times; that is, if
                    <m>T</m> is the matrix transformation that rotates vectors
                    by <m>90^\circ</m> find the matrix for
                    <m>T\circ T \circ T \circ T</m>.
                    Explain why your result makes sense geometrically.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The matrix that results from composing a <m>90^\circ</m>
                    rotation with itself is a rotation by <m>180^\circ</m>.
                    If <m>C</m> from part (a) is the matrix for a
                    <m>90^\circ</m> rotation, then <m>C \circ C = C^2</m> is the
                    matrix for a rotation by <m>180^\circ</m>.
                    Thus,
                    <md>
                        C^2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    The matrix for <m>T\circ T \circ T \circ T</m> is
                    <md>
                        C^4 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ \end{bmatrix},
                    </md>
                    which is the identity matrix!
                    Therefore, if we rotate by <m>90^\circ</m> four times, this
                    is rotating by <m>360^\circ</m>, which takes us back to the
                    original position and thus, there isn't really any rotation.
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
C = matrix(2,2,[0,-1,
                1,0])
C*C
                    </input>
                    <output>
[-1  0]
[ 0 -1]
                    </output>
                </sage>
                <sage>
                    <input>
C = matrix(2,2,[0,-1,
                1,0])
C^4
                    </input>
                    <output>
[1 0]
[0 1]
                    </output>
                </sage>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare the result of rotating by <m>90^\circ</m> and then
                    reflecting over the line <m>y=x</m> to the result of first
                    reflecting over <m>y=x</m> and then rotating
                    <m>90^\circ</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Rotating by <m>90^\circ</m> then reflecting over <m>y=x</m>
                    is <m>B\cdot C</m> (where <m>B</m> is the matrix from
                    Problem 5(b)).
                    We apply <m>C</m> to <m>\xvec</m> first and then, <m>B</m>.
                    Here,
                    <md>
                        B\cdot C = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \\ \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Reflecting over <m>y=x</m> and then rotating by
                    <m>90^\circ</m> is <m>C\cdot B</m>, where
                    <md>
                        C\cdot B = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>
                <sage>
                    <input>
#Reflect over y=x
B = matrix(2,2,[0,1,
                1,0])
#Rotate 90 counterclockwise
C = matrix(2,2,[0,-1,
                1,0])
print("B*C = rotate then reflect")
print(B*C)
print("C*B = reflect then rotate")
print(C*B)
                    </input>
                    <output>
B*C = rotate then reflect
[ 1  0]
[ 0 -1]
C*B = reflect then rotate
[-1  0]
[ 0  1]
                    </output>
                </sage>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do matrix transformations "commute"?
                    That is, if <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> are two transformations,
                    is <m>S\circ T = T\circ S</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    No!
                    Rotate then reflect is different than reflect and then
                    rotate.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For example,
                    <md>
                        \twovec{1}{0} \xrightarrow{\textrm{rotate}} \twovec{0}{1} \xrightarrow{\textrm{reflect}} \twovec{1}{0},
                    </md>
                    whereas
                    <md>
                        \twovec{1}{0} \xrightarrow{\textrm{reflect}} \twovec{0}{1} \xrightarrow{\textrm{rotate}} \twovec{-1}{0},
                    </md>
                    and these aren't equal!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain why the matrix that rotates vectors counterclockwise by
                an angle <m>\theta</m> is
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta \amp-\sin\theta\\
\sin\theta \amp\cos\theta
\end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We look at the action of the transformation on our vectors
                <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                So,
                <md>
                    T(\evec_1) = T\left(\twovec{1}{0}\right) = \twovec{\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)}. \\
                </md>
                This is because if we take <m>\evec_1</m> and rotate it by the
                angle theta, then the <m>x</m>-component is the horizontal
                component of the triangle, which is <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and the
                <m>y</m>-component is the vertical component of the triangle,
                which is <m>\sin(\theta)</m>.
                See blue triangle in diagram below.
                For <m>\evec_2</m>, we have
                <md>
                    T(\evec_2) = T\left(\twovec{0}{1}\right) = \twovec{-\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}. \\
                </md>
                Here, the horizontal component of the resulting vector is
                <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, but in the negative <m>x</m>-direction,
                making the component <m>-\sin(\theta)</m> and the vertical
                component is <m>\cos(\theta)</m>.
                See green triangle in diagram below.
            </p>

            <image source="images/14-rotation01.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Linear transformation that rotates vectors counterclockwise
                    by an angle theta.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the matrix for a transformation that scales
                    horizontally by a factor of <m>a</m> and vertically by a
                    factor of <m>b</m>?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the matrix for a transformation that rotates
                    counterclockwise by an angle <m>\theta</m>?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How can you find the matrix associated with the composition
                    of two matrix transformations?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>